  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Archaeologists keep unearthing hollow bronze 12-sided objects across Europe. These Roman artifacts feature knobs on every corner and distinct holes on each face.",
        "visual_query": "roman dodecahedron bronze artifact europe",
        "visual_fallback": "ancient bronze artifact"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Over a hundred exist across Britain, France, and Germany. Yet across millions of surviving Roman documents, not one text mentions them.",
        "visual_query": "roman ruins archaeology britain france",
        "visual_fallback": "ancient stone ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Were they sacred icons or over-engineered tools? None show wax or wear marks, disproving theories they were candleholders or dice.",
        "visual_query": "roman bronze museum display artifact",
        "visual_fallback": "museum display case"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Some researchers suggested their geometry could form a jig for knitting gloves, but their true purpose remains an unsolved mystery.",
        "visual_query": "woolen glove knitting hand weaving",
        "visual_fallback": "hands weaving fabric"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They were used almost exclusively along Rome\u2019s northern European frontier. But if they were so widespread, why did no Roman ever write down their name?",
        "visual_query": "roman fort snowy winter europe",
        "visual_fallback": "snowy ancient landscape"
      }
    ],
